[
  {
    "question": "Which of the following statements about the Salt March of 1930 is/are correct? \n1. It was a pivotal event in the Civil Disobedience Movement led by Mahatma Gandhi to challenge British laws on salt. \n2. The movement saw significant participation from women and the business community, reflecting a broader societal engagement against colonial rule. \n3. The Salt March was primarily focused on the promotion of khadi and local panchayats rather than direct action against the British government. \n4. The movement was halted after the Chauri Chaura incident and did not resume until the signing of the Gandhi-Irwin Pact in 1931.",
    "options": [
      "A. Only statements 1 and 2 are correct.",
      "B. Statements 1, 2, and 4 are correct.",
      "C. Only statement 3 is correct.",
      "D. All statements are correct."
    ],
    "answer": "B. Statements 1, 2, and 4 are correct.",
    "explanation": "Statement 1 is correct as the Salt March was indeed a pivotal event aimed at breaking British salt laws. Statement 2 is correct because the movement saw increased participation from women and the business community. Statement 4 is correct as the movement was suspended after the Gandhi-Irwin Pact in 1931, before relaunching later. Statement 3 is incorrect; while the promotion of khadi was part of the movement's broader strategy, the Salt March was fundamentally a direct action against British salt laws rather than just focused on constructive programs. Pro-Tip: Understand the interplay between various actions in the freedom struggle to better analyze multi-faceted movements."
